 r"I am writing to apply for the Graduate Product Analyst position at Wise. I am currently completing an MSc in Health Data Science at University College London, after graduating from Imperial College London with a BSc in Biological Sciences. Across my academic and professional experience I have worked on defining what to measure, running experiments to test whether a change helped, and turning the result into a recommendation someone could act on. This role is close to what I do now: I work as a Data Analyst alongside e-commerce teams, building their KPIs and dashboards and answering their questions with SQL and Python.",

 r"What attracts me most to Wise is that the analysis has a visible end point. The posting describes analysts embedded in a Product team, setting up and analysing experiments for every significant release, and helping product managers, engineers and designers decide what to change next. That is the part of analytics I want to do. I enjoy taking an unclear business problem, identifying what users actually need, and turning it into a workflow that can be tested and improved.",

 r"At TikTok Shop UK I mine merchant and transaction data in SQL and Python to identify the growth and operations drivers behind the UK marketplace. I build the KPI frameworks and dashboards that e-commerce teams use to set targets and track their initiatives, and I present what I find to operations, product and commercial stakeholders. The recurring lesson has been that the first metric someone asks for is rarely the one that answers their question, so I try to agree what to measure before building anything. I also built an AI agent with Claude Code that automates repetitive steps of the analytics workflow, now adopted by my own team and by partner teams. Building it meant understanding an existing workflow in detail and then supporting colleagues as they began to rely on it.",

 r"As an Applied AI Engineer at DS Digital Technology I worked for a pharmaceutical client that needed a way to judge whether its patient-facing AI answers were good enough. I designed a 246-question benchmark and a six-dimension rubric that the client adopted as its acceptance standard, built a Python pipeline that produced and scored more than 2,400 responses across six models, and ran blinded A/B tests to check whether a medically reviewed knowledge graph improved answer quality. Designing the measure before running the test is the habit I would bring to experiment analysis at Wise.",

 r"My MSc dissertation used data from 458,840 UK Biobank participants to build cardiovascular risk models, then estimated how lifestyle changes affect that risk using causal methods, with sensitivity analyses for unmeasured confounding. Much of the work was deciding which differences in the data were real and which came from who had been measured and when, which is a question that comes up in product data as well. I delivered the results as an interactive Streamlit app so that people could explore them without reading the code.",

 r"At Ipsos Strategy3 I analysed data from more than 80 healthcare professional interviews alongside market datasets, and turned the findings into visualisations and recommendations for client stakeholders. That is where I learned to present a finding to an audience who wants the implication rather than the method, and to adjust how I present it depending on who is listening.",

 r"I would bring Wise strong SQL and Python, practical experience defining KPIs and building dashboards, a solid grounding in experiment design and statistics, and the habit of checking whether a result holds before recommending it.",

